A||||11||fierospeeder||Ford Exploreres dont roll over!!||||1||
Z||000000||fierospeeder||12-09-2000||06:08 PM||||Its saturday, my favorite day because crank and chrome, trucks are on TNN cable and i love to tape them.  While rewinding my video tape because im getting low on room.  I saw car and driver on my tape.<P>Car and driver did a test with a ford explorer.  They bought a used explorer from a used car lot and did no performance mods to it.  They hooked up a special device on the drivers side rear tire that would deflate the tire in a very quick rate.  They also put an air compressor in the truck to inflate the tire when the test was over.  A solenoid was used to deflate the tire and this was done remote control by someone on the track.<P>This was done at a dragstrip, and the driver starts at 20mph and the tire gets deflated like a blowout and the explorer maintains direction, going in a straight direction.<BR>30,40,50,60,70 and all the blow outs had no effect on the driving ability of the explorer.  At 70, the driver said its just a usual blowout, you brake gently and come to a stop.  He also tried a test with stopping on the brake and it still stayed on the road.<P><BR>So does this test say that firestone tires didn't cause the explorers to roll over???? thats what they were trying to prove.<P>on 20/20, they stated that most people that drive SUVs, dont really read their owners manual and tend to "overload" their vehicle.  They also did a road test on that, and the professional driver stated that handeling is very poor when the vehicle has weight added in it, making the body tend to "roll."<P>Anyone have opinions?  <BR>I had a flat on my tbird, didn't realize it because it was on the passenger side rear.  My car was very stable and i thought it was transmission problem, my trans stumbled really loud before just like my flat did, one of the valves was stuck. Thats after a rebuild by a mechanic, problem solved piece of metal got it stuck.  I still dont thing i got overdrive.<P>Were the people who rolled over, "overloaded their Suvs" and reacted poorly when there firestone tires seperated into two pieces??<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||RNGCRSH||12-09-2000||07:08 PM||||I've seen this topic once before. But anyways, A tread seperation and a blowout are two totaly different things, what thier jig did not simulate is a 3 ft long piece of rubber swinging at 70mph cutting brake lines and locking up suspension components. they should have tried a front drivers side blowout. if you have ever seen a 18 wheeler tire seperate and the damage it does to the Trailer. This happen in an 18 wheeler for one of 2 reasons improper loading (too much weight on any givin axle) or improper inflation (under inlated tires run hotter)<P>Firestone sugested to Ford to run the tires on explorers at 35psi Ford insisted in the owners manual to inflate to 26psi (this is according to the last news report I watched on the subject) if this is true then Ford is entirely responsible for all fatalities from these tread seperation accidents.<P>The big auto makers will always try to blame someone else for their mistakes!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||12-10-2000||12:25 AM||||I seen that show before too.<P>I'm thinking most of the time the separated tread isn't always to blame.  I watched a video of an Explorer, and seen the tread slinging off the tire, the Explorer never skipped a beat or with any dramatic loss of control.  I feel the problem is people are over-reacting to the "PoP" of the blowout and panic.  Chances are they are dropping their cell phones and giving the wheel a good whip causing the endos.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-10-2000||12:29 AM||||We've done this thread before.<P>The C&D simulation does not accuratly reflect conditions of a blow out OR tread sep. If they really wanted to simulate a blow out they needed to contact a stunt company and buy a bunch of tires. The stunt company would have the equipment and licensed pyrotechnicians to do this job correctly and legally. They also need a differant test site that allows testing on various curves and straights. Watkins Glen in NY would be a much better test than a dragway.<P>Blowout comletely deflates the tire in a few Milliseconds. Blowout is always accompanied by severe tire damage, usually to sidewalls. I've personally seen sidewalls of tires split around the entire circumfrence of the tire. (This type of sidewall split can be caused by tire aging but is most often a result of low air pressure.)<P>The firestone Tread sep problem results in the entire tread And the Steel belt layer comming apart. This debris is extremely strong and can bind the wheel to the equivelant of slamming the brake on the effected wheel. In many cases these tires did not deflate at all or not untill after the wheel locked and the road surface cut the rest of the cording out.<P>Anyone that really thinks Explorers don't roll needs to do allot more reasearch. As a class SUV's are some of the most unstable vehicles ever built. Ford's own documants have stated that the reason for the reduced air pressure was to supposedly improve the stability of the vehicle.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-10-2000).]||||reg||8
Z||000004||jlhuber||12-10-2000||02:09 AM||||This reply has very little to do with the tire blowout/separation, only SUVs.  A lot of the "tip over" factor has to due with the fact that SUVs are top heavy.  It has been long known in the 4wheelin' community that to make a 4x4 more stable you have to widen the stance as much as you raise the 4x4 up.  It actually applies to all vehicles, like the Pontiac Grand Prix commercials say, "wider is better".  Most SUVs are too narrow for their height, causing them to tip over under cornering or off-roading.  Now if you were to widen the stance it solves part of the problem.  But it all falls back on the driver.  I agree that most SUV roll overs are a result of the driver not knowing how to handle the vehicle.  AARRGGHH!!!Example: soccer mom has driven a stationwagon most of her married life, but this year she decides to jump on the bandwagon and be trendy like all the other soccer moms and buy a big, ugly, gas hogg, SUV (super ugly vehicle).  First off, the SUV is not a 'wagon and it does not have the handling characteristics of one.  Thus, you can't take corners as fast trying to get in-front of the car that is right next to you just so you can turn onto a different road as soon as you get in front like you could in the lower-to-the-ground, more stable 'wagon.  And another thing, turn that damned cell phone off and concentrate on driving so I won't have to kick your A$$ for crashing into my car!  You could atleast give it to the passenger, or if ti is that important, pull over to the side of the road and talk or have the passenger drive.  This is not directed to offend anyone, and it is not meant to degrate women.  So please, do not take it that way.  I am just expressing my hate for SUVs, driving-while-talking-on-cell-phone people, richy beeotches, and stupid drivers (male and female).  Ahhh...I love venting.  Later   <P><BR>oops, sorry.  This should have been posted under the thread "SUV", but I read this one first<BR> <BR>------------------<BR>--Jess--<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/blackeye2/double0smile.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by jlhuber (edited 12-10-2000).]||||reg||8
