solved piece of metal got it stuck.  I still dont thing i got overdrive.<P>Were the people who rolled over, "overloaded their Suvs" and reacted poorly when there firestone tires seperated into two pieces??</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Dont forget "My Classic Car" and "HotRod TV"......... I love Sat. also  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I try not to miss the shows..<P>They replay the shows twice on Sun. to... Im watching My Classic Car right now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/goodlogo1_1.gif">    <BR><B>"AHHHHH!!! All Fiero's don't start on fire you BONEHEAD!!"</B>||||reg||14
Z||000006||Slammed Fiero||12-11-2000||11:48 AM||||The tire on the explorer has been out for a while , if you look at the #'s for accidents involving tire malfunction , Vs Number of tires made and installed on the explorer , the rate of incident involving the tires is less than 1% , Just the media likes to hype things up , propaganda and made it seem like every truck is rolling over. Remember the corvair .. Swing axle design 60 to 64 , same suspension design as VW beetle which ralph nader was going after next. Same thing media went nuts. What SUV doesn't roll over , the center of gravity is extremely high on them and most people think they handle like a car...<BR>Oh yeah My uncle owns a SVT dealership , thats where I got the info<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pontiaddict||12-11-2000||08:47 PM||||On one of the last runs. They show the car from the front drivers side. When the tire goes, the front end moves to the right about 8 inches. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phantom Rage||12-12-2000||04:03 AM||||Ok, was it just me or did they only simulate a tire blow out from only the rear of the vehicle?  Why did they not blow out the front?  I would assume that Explorers have the majority of the weight bias on the front half of the vehicle, where it is more likely a tire blow out would occur.<P>This is in now way negatively aimed at the Ford Explorer.<P>I am just perplexed as to why Car and Driver did not do a more thorough test.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Phantom Rage (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000009||Phil||12-12-2000||07:04 AM||||I was yaking with my tire guy the other day and he wonders why it's always the right rear  tire that goes on the Explorer. Makes you think that there is more to the story than just Firestones||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||12-14-2000||07:55 PM||||blowing a tire driving in a straight line shouldnt be any trouble for anyone. trouble is most panic and jam on the brakes or turn quick to the shoulder. Hwever if it blows on a curve, you could def have your handsfull. Watch tv nascar guys can have blowouts on straights over 200 mph and keep control, but in a curve-u have to pray a little. Most of the people i see driving suvs on freeway are going over 80-90 mph and dont have a clue how to drive anyway. If I see one coming i stay in right lane and let them get wayyyyyyyyy ahead of me - or sttomp the gas and leave em back a few miles<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||WampusCat||12-14-2000||08:17 PM||||There are a few more details in the Jan C&D.  They said they chose the left rear because "statistically, it's the most likey position for the reported separations."  The deflating device they used releases all the tire pressure in 1/3 second according to them.  On one of the 70 mph runs, the driver held both hands up and off the wheel before and during the simulated blowout.  "The explorer didn't veer or pull.  Only when I applied the brakes toward the end of the run did the explorer pull enough to require me to put my hands back on the wheel."||||reg||1
